<section aria-labelledby="about-tool" class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 pt-16">
  <article class="prose-site">
    <h2 id="about-tool" style="margin-top:0">What is a rolling offset?</h2>
    <p>A rolling offset moves a run of pipe or conduit up and sideways at the same time. The run ends on a new line that is parallel to the old one. You need it when a beam, a duct, a wall or another pipe is in the way and the path is not flat or square. This rolling offset calculator gives you every number you need to lay it out: the true offset, the travel, the run and the cut length.</p>

    <h2>What each number means</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rise</strong> is the vertical distance between the two center lines.</li>
      <li><strong>Roll</strong> is the sideways distance between the two center lines.</li>
      <li><strong>True offset</strong> (the set) is the straight distance between the center lines. It is the diagonal across the rise and the roll.</li>
      <li><strong>Travel</strong> is the center to center length of the angled piece between the two fittings.</li>
      <li><strong>Run</strong> is how far the offset moves along the pipe. It is the length the offset uses up on the straight line.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to use the calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Measure the rise and the roll from center line to center line, in inches.</li>
      <li>Type them in. Use decimals such as 12.5, or fractions such as 12 1/2. The fraction buttons add 1/8 inch steps on a phone.</li>
      <li>Pick the fitting angle: 22.5, 45 or 60 degrees, or type your own.</li>
      <li>For pipe, pick the pipe type and size to subtract the fitting take-off. The tool then shows the cut length.</li>
      <li>For EMT, switch to conduit mode and pick the bend angle. The tool shows the distance between bends and the shrink.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Which fitting angle should you use?</h2>
    <p>Most plumbers use 45 degree fittings. They are easy to stock, and they keep the offset short. A 22.5 degree fitting makes a long, gentle offset that is good for drain lines. A 60 degree fitting makes a short, steep offset when space is tight, but it slows the flow more. A smaller angle always makes a longer travel for the same offset. Try each angle in the tool and compare the run.</p>

    <h2>The formulas</h2>
    <p>The math is right-triangle geometry. These are the steps the calculator follows.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>True offset = the square root of (rise squared + roll squared)</li>
      <li>Travel = true offset / sin(angle)</li>
      <li>Run = true offset / tan(angle)</li>
      <li>Cut length = travel - 2 x take-off</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>A copper line must clear a duct. The rise is 12 inches and the roll is 9 inches. You use 45 degree fittings.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>True offset = the square root of (144 + 81) = 15 inches.</li>
      <li>Travel = 15 / sin 45 = 21.21 inches, or about 21 3/16 inches.</li>
      <li>Run = 15 / tan 45 = 15 inches.</li>
      <li>The 3/4 inch copper fitting has a take-off of about 3/8 inch. Cut length = 21.21 - 0.75 = 20.46 inches, or about 20 7/16 inches.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Common rise and roll values</h2>
    <p>These pairs give a whole number for the true offset. The travel is for 45 degree fittings.</p>
