    <h2>Rolling offset in EMT conduit</h2>
    <p>Conduit is bent, not fitted, so the rules change a little. The bender angle sets two numbers. The multiplier is one divided by the sine of the angle. Multiply the true offset by it to get the distance between the two bends. Shrink is the length the conduit loses when you bend it. It equals the true offset times the tangent of half the angle.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption class="sr-only">Multiplier and shrink per inch of offset by bend angle</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Bend angle</th><th scope="col">Multiplier</th><th scope="col">Shrink per inch of offset</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>10&deg;</td><td>5.76</td><td>0.087 in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>22.5&deg;</td><td>2.61</td><td>0.199 in (about 3/16)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30&deg;</td><td>2.00</td><td>0.268 in (about 1/4)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>45&deg;</td><td>1.41</td><td>0.414 in (about 3/8)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>60&deg;</td><td>1.15</td><td>0.577 in</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Example: the true offset is 15 inches and you bend at 30 degrees. The bends go 30 inches apart. The shrink is about 4 inches. The "offset direction" line in the tool tells you how far to rotate the conduit between the two bends, measured from the rise direction.</p>

    <h2>Tips for a clean job</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Measure to the center of the pipe, not to the edge. For conduit, measure to the center of the tube.</li>
      <li>Use the same fitting angle on both ends of the offset.</li>
      <li>Check the take-off against the fittings in your hand. Sizes differ between makers.</li>
      <li>Dry fit the piece before you glue, solder or weld.</li>
      <li>If the cut length comes out zero or negative, the fittings are too large for that offset. Use a smaller angle or a longer rise and roll.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
